From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Steffen Deusch <steffen@deusch.me>
Date: Fri, 3 Apr 2026 21:14:07 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] (halium) skip SELinux context check for decompressed APEXes

apexd checks the SELinux label of a freshly decompressed APEX. On Halium
this always fails: restorecon is stubbed out, and the container runs
either under AppArmor or under SELinux permissive with no policy loaded.
getfilecon() then returns ENODATA, the decompressed file is discarded,
and the compressed APEX silently stays inactive while apexd still
reports "ready".

is_selinux_enabled() alone is not enough as a guard: libselinux returns
true whenever selinuxfs is mounted, which it is in the container even
without a policy. Only run the check when SELinux is enforcing, where a
wrong label would actually matter.

Change-Id: I7b1c1116b60a2ccea11367333a649c11c7f340d4
---
 apexd/apexd.cpp | 17 +++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/apexd/apexd.cpp b/apexd/apexd.cpp
index 2eefe175..181ef771 100644
--- a/apexd/apexd.cpp
+++ b/apexd/apexd.cpp
@@ -2462,12 +2462,17 @@ Result<ApexFile> OpenAndValidateDecompressedApex(const ApexFile& capex,
   if (!result.ok()) {
     return result.error();
   }
-  auto ctx = GetfileconPath(apex_path);
-  if (!ctx.ok()) {
-    return ctx.error();
-  }
-  if (!StartsWith(*ctx, gConfig->active_apex_selinux_ctx)) {
-    return Error() << apex_path << " has wrong SELinux context " << *ctx;
+  // Only check the label when SELinux can enforce it. Halium runs the
+  // container permissive without a policy, so the decompressed file has
+  // no label and getfilecon() fails with ENODATA.
+  if (is_selinux_enabled() && security_getenforce() == 1) {
+    auto ctx = GetfileconPath(apex_path);
+    if (!ctx.ok()) {
+      return ctx.error();
+    }
+    if (!StartsWith(*ctx, gConfig->active_apex_selinux_ctx)) {
+      return Error() << apex_path << " has wrong SELinux context " << *ctx;
+    }
   }
   return std::move(*apex);
 }
